    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, state, attempt, next_attempt_at,\n          lease_until, provider_msg_id, sent_at, idem_key UNIQUE, updated_at)\n```\n\n- `start_at_utc` is the source of truth; `tz` is stored for display and for computing \"9am local\" style rules.\n- `idem_key = hash(appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc)` \u2014 one logical reminder per (appointment, channel, fire time). This is the dedup key.\n- `state \u2208 {pending, leased, sent, failed, cancelled}`.\n\n## Scheduling and time zones\n\n- All scheduling math is done in UTC. `fire_at_utc = start_at_utc - lead_time`.\n- The appointment's `tz` is used only to render the human-readable time in the message body (\"Your appointment is Tue 3:00 PM PDT\").\n- DST is handled by storing the appointment as an absolute instant. If a rule is \"remind at 9am local the day before,\" compute that local wall time in the appointment's tz, convert to UTC once, and store it. Never recompute from wall time at send time.\n- If the appointment's tz changes (patient moves), that's an explicit update that recomputes `fire_at_utc` and bumps `version",
